WEBVTT
Language: en

1
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:17.700
Earth’s axis is tilted 23.44° from the normal to its orbit and points the same way all year, so the north end leans toward the Sun in June and away from it in December. Sizes and distances are not to scale.

2
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:34.267
In June the north end leans toward the Sun. London’s noon Sun is high, and most of its latitude circle, the line through the dot, is on the day side, which gives 16.4 h of daylight.

3
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:53.267
Zoomed in on the red dot. The white line is London’s horizon, and noon sunlight arrives parallel to the Sun’s direction. The angle between them is 90° minus the gap between the latitude, 51.5°, and the latitude where the June Sun is overhead, 23.44°.

4
00:00:57.333 --> 00:01:15.333
Half an orbit later Earth is on the far side of the Sun, but the axis still points the same way, so the south end now leans sunward and the hemispheres swap seasons. In the marked row, London’s daylight drops from 16.4 h in June to 7.6 h.

5
00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:37.233
Each line is one place’s latitude circle, yellow where it is in daylight. Tromsø’s circle is all day in June and all night in December, while the equator’s is always cut in half. The model’s day length in hours is acos(−tan latitude × tan declination) in degrees, divided by 7.5.

6
00:01:44.700 --> 00:02:03.833
The curve samples the same model through the year, with the Sun’s declination on each day; the card’s counts run it on all 365 days. London’s day grows from 7.6 h at the December solstice to 16.4 h at the June solstice. Every place gets 12 hours at the two equinoxes.

7
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:26.600
Earth is nearest the Sun in early January. On 15 January London gets 8.0 h of daylight and latitude -51.5°, the second curve, gets 16.0 h. Both are the same distance from the Sun that day, so the opposite seasons must come from the tilt.

8
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:41.933
The chart runs the same model at four latitudes. Tromsø’s curve sits flat at 24 h and at 0 h for weeks, London and Sydney peak six months apart, and Quito stays at 12 hours.
